Phase 1 · Student & System (Profile & Competency Baseline)
Student Registration & Profile: Student creates an account, specifies weekly availability hours (5–40 hrs/week), preferred technical role (Frontend, Backend, ML/AI, Hardware, or Coordinator), and background interests.
Portfolio Building: Student showcases GitHub repositories, live demo URLs, tech stack tags, certifications (e.g., AWS, TensorFlow), and honors.
General Competency Calculation: The transparent scoring engine computes verified individual scores:

Phase 2 · Coordinator (Event Configuration)
Configure & Publish Competition: The coordinator defines team sizes (
), total teams (
), registration deadlines, allowed score gap (
), and skill weights (
 from 0 to 10).
Phase 3 · Student (Discovery & Teammate Preferences)
Discover & Apply: Students review active events, inspect their calculated fit and eligibility (mandatory skill requirements), and submit voluntary applications.
Submit Teammate Preferences: Students declare whether they wish to volunteer as a team leader and optionally designate up to 3 peers to prefer working with or avoid (separation requests).
Phase 4 · Algorithmic Engine (Optimization & Team Generation)
Competition-Specific Fitness: The engine recalculates event-specific fit:
Leadership Selection: Volunteer leaders are ranked by leadership suitability:

Qualified leaders are seeded one-per-team into distinct candidate rosters.
Candidate Team Generation: The balancer executes 8 restarts of 2,500 swap search iterations against an objective function penalizing score spread past tolerance, broken preferences, and skill coverage gaps:
Phase 5 · Coordinator & Student (Review & Finalization)
Review Proposed Teams: The coordinator inspects Option 1, Option 2, and Option 3 tabs with spread checks, teammate wishes met counts, separation request audits, and interactive bar charts.
Validate and Finalize: On approval, the team rosters are locked into the database, generating private team workspaces for all confirmed students.
Phase 6 · Team (Private Workspace & Sprint Management)
Collaborative Workspace: Confirmed members collaborate through:
Sprint Completion Tracker: Real-time progress bar.
Member Workload Bar Chart: Planned estimated hours per member.
Milestone Manager: Key target deadlines with overdue tracking.
3-Column Kanban Board: To do, In progress, and Done cards with assignee permissions.
Phase 7 · Team & Coordinator (Deliverables, Audit & Closure)
Project Submission & Closure:
Teams submit their project title, GitHub repository link, architecture notes, and demo link.
The coordinator reviews submitted code, assigns a judge score (0–100), provides qualitative feedback, benchmarks fairness against a 300-trial Monte Carlo random assignment, downloads the audit CSV, and marks the event Completed.
3. Features Implemented in the Live App
Portfolio & Skill DNA Tab:
Students can manage their GitHub profile, bio, and add projects with tags and demo links.
Interactive skill sliders for Proficiency (1–5) and Experience (0–10) that dynamically update individual scores.
13-Step Workflow Guide:
A dedicated "13-Step Workflow" button in the header opens a full phase-by-phase modal detailing the entire platform flow.
Phase 7 Project Submission & Coordinator Grading:
Teams can submit deliverables in their workspace.
Coordinators can grade projects, provide feedback, and close the event.
Interactive Role Switching:
Switch between Prof. Sharma (Coordinator) and any student (e.g. Aarav, Diya) via the header account switcher to test both perspectives.
